1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,241
Night at the pumps.

2
00:00:03,541 --> 00:00:08,251
Inside, the counter, the kitchen, the coolers and the aisles keep moving.

3
00:00:08,551 --> 00:00:13,273
This is one store, and every hour it asks its owner for another decision.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,573
Most of those decisions arrive separately.

5
00:00:28,873 --> 00:00:30,716
Fuel numbers in one place.

6
00:00:31,016 --> 00:00:32,512
Shelf counts in another.

7
00:00:32,812 --> 00:00:37,018
The register, the kitchen, the shift notes, each with its own story.

8
00:00:37,318 --> 00:00:40,559
So the owner ends up chasing problems one at a time,

9
00:00:40,559 --> 00:00:43,363
often after they have already cost something.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,292
Start with the cooler.

11
00:00:52,592 --> 00:00:57,086
The old way, a shortage gets noticed when the shelf is already empty,

12
00:00:57,086 --> 00:01:00,278
and a customer leaves without what they came for.

13
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:07,990
PI StationOS is designed to change that order of events.

14
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:15,554
In this simulation, it brings sales pace and stock on hand together, sees a drink selling faster than usual,

15
00:01:15,554 --> 00:01:19,993
and flags that it is likely to run short before the next delivery.

16
00:01:20,293 --> 00:01:22,957
It explains why, and drafts a reorder.

17
00:01:23,257 --> 00:01:24,971
Nothing is sent on its own.

18
00:01:25,271 --> 00:01:28,952
The owner reviews it, changes the quantity, and approves.

19
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:33,148
Now step back outside.

20
00:01:33,448 --> 00:01:37,249
Fuel and inside sales are usually reviewed apart.

21
00:01:37,549 --> 00:01:41,545
Put them in one operating picture, and the decision changes.

22
00:01:41,845 --> 00:01:47,264
In this example, evening pump traffic is up, but inside sales have not followed.

23
00:01:47,565 --> 00:01:51,302
Looking at food sales alone, you might cut back on prep.

24
00:01:51,602 --> 00:01:53,119
With the pumps in view,

25
00:01:53,119 --> 00:01:57,935
the better move is to staff the counter, and invite those drivers inside.

26
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,950
Then comes the shift change, where details slip.

27
00:02:09,250 --> 00:02:12,382
PI proposes a clear handoff: what was finished,

28
00:02:12,382 --> 00:02:16,048
what is still open, and the one exception that matters.

29
00:02:16,348 --> 00:02:18,340
Here, the drawer count does not match.

30
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:20,916
The next person gets a specific action,

31
00:02:20,916 --> 00:02:23,133
and the owner can see when it is done.

32
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:26,802
And every recommendation comes to you.

33
00:02:27,102 --> 00:02:29,315
Approve it, edit it, or decline it.

34
00:02:29,615 --> 00:02:30,916
You stay informed.

35
00:02:31,216 --> 00:02:32,633
You stay in control.

36
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:41,749
PI StationOS is a proposed way to help owners run the whole station,

37
00:02:41,749 --> 00:02:47,152
fuel, shelves, counter and shifts, with more clarity and more control.

38
00:02:47,452 --> 00:02:49,618
It is not connected to this store today.

39
00:02:49,918 --> 00:02:52,537
We want to build it with owners who know the work.

40
00:02:53,157 --> 00:02:54,271
See the concept.

41
00:02:54,891 --> 00:02:56,041
Shape the pilot.

42
00:02:56,661 --> 00:02:57,863
Ask for a demo.
